_os1(_cL,_dL,YYASSET_REF(0x000001F2),"dialog","Charles...",1);_os1(_cL,_dL,YYASSET_REF(0x0000000D),"dialog","What about the lines? They're black. They divide the ball into little segments and they improve grip.",1);_os1(_cL,_dL,YYASSET_REF(0x0000000D),"dialog","???:\\Ahhh, the segments. Did you know those lines are the reason I got in this business?");_os1(_cL,_dL,YYASSET_REF(0x0000000D),"dialog","What do you mean? What are you talking about...",1);_os1(_cL,_dL,YYASSET_REF(0x0000000D),"dialog","???:\\It's true. You see, those lines, they mean different things to you than they do to me. You mentioned that the lines divide the ball into segments. I believe you are wrong, Charles Barkley. I believe that the lines are in fact a symbol of unity, that they are the only things holding the ball together. Have you ever considered that even though the lines all go in different directions, they all have the exact same point of origin and end? Have you ever considered that without those lines, you'd be staring at a bunch of strips of leather and rubber and not a basketball? They bring the ball together. That is why I made basketballs, Charles Barkley. To bring people together, to show them that the lines on a b-ball do not divide it, but hold it together. I suppose I failed.");
